    mostLikelyDiagnoses:
      'A stereotyped headache preceded by a zig-zag shimmering visual aura lasting 20-30 minutes, with a normal eye exam, is most consistent with migraine. Headache with severe eye pain, halos around lights, nausea, and a red eye with an elevated IOP and shallow angle indicates acute angle closure glaucoma. New-onset headache in a patient over 50 with scalp tenderness, jaw claudication, and/or visual symptoms indicates presumed giant cell arteritis until laboratory and biopsy results say otherwise. Headache with bilateral optic disc edema (papilledema), transient visual graying with posture change, and pulsatile tinnitus in a young, often overweight woman suggests idiopathic intracranial hypertension. Headache with diplopia, ptosis, and a dilated pupil suggests a compressive CN III palsy and warrants emergent evaluation for an intracranial aneurysm. Headache with neck pain following trauma or a chiropractic manipulation, accompanied by Horner syndrome, raises concern for carotid artery dissection.',
