    mostLikelyDiagnoses:
      'Diplopia that persists with one eye covered is monocular diplopia and is almost always an optical or ocular-surface problem — irregular astigmatism, cataract, or a subluxed lens — rather than a neurologic one. True binocular diplopia that resolves with either eye covered points to a misalignment of the visual axes. Vertical or oblique diplopia worse looking down and toward the nose, often with a head tilt to the opposite shoulder, suggests a CN IV (trochlear) palsy. Horizontal diplopia worse at distance with an inability to abduct the affected eye suggests a CN VI (abducens) palsy, commonly microvascular in older patients with diabetes/hypertension but also seen with raised intracranial pressure. Diplopia with ptosis and the eye deviated down and out suggests a CN III palsy — pupil involvement raises concern for compressive aneurysm, while pupil-sparing presentations in older vasculopathic patients are more often microvascular. Fluctuating diplopia and ptosis that worsen with fatigue and improve with rest suggests ocular myasthenia gravis. Diplopia with restricted eye movement (classically limited upgaze), proptosis, lid retraction, and eyelid edema suggests thyroid eye disease. Diplopia after facial/orbital trauma with restricted upgaze suggests an orbital blowout fracture with extraocular muscle entrapment.',
    urgency: 'Urgent',
    managementConsiderations:
      'New binocular diplopia warrants prompt evaluation to localize the cause and exclude dangerous etiologies; a pupil-involving CN III palsy is a same-day emergency requiring neuroimaging (CT/MR angiography) and neurosurgical consultation to rule out a compressive aneurysm. An isolated, pupil-sparing CN III, IV, or VI palsy in an older patient with vascular risk factors can often be managed as a presumed microvascular palsy with close interval follow-up (expecting improvement over 3 months), but new neuroimaging is warranted if it fails to improve, worsens, or is accompanied by other neurologic signs. Suspected myasthenia gravis warrants prompt referral for confirmatory testing (e.g., acetylcholine receptor antibodies, edrophonium or ice-pack test, single-fiber EMG) and screening for systemic/bulbar involvement given its potential to affect respiration. Orbital blowout fracture with entrapment, especially in children with a "trapdoor" fracture and oculocardiac reflex symptoms (nausea, bradycardia), needs urgent oculoplastic/orbital surgery evaluation. Thyroid eye disease with compressive optic neuropathy (decreased vision, color desaturation, RAPD) is an emergency requiring urgent orbital imaging and likely high-dose corticosteroids or orbital decompression. Monocular diplopia is generally managed routinely by addressing the underlying refractive or ocular-surface cause.',
