    mostLikelyDiagnoses:
      'Deep, boring pain that wakes the patient at night, with diffuse or nodular non-blanching scleral injection and tenderness to palpation over the globe, points to scleritis, which carries a meaningful association with underlying systemic autoimmune disease and, in its necrotizing form, threat to ocular integrity. Milder, more localized, non-tender redness with mild-to-moderate discomfort that blanches with phenylephrine is more consistent with episcleritis, a generally benign and self-limited condition. Aching pain with photophobia, a ciliary/circumlimbal flush, and anterior chamber cell/flare on slit-lamp exam points to anterior uveitis. Severe pain with nausea, halos, a steamy cornea, and markedly elevated IOP in a shallow angle indicates acute angle closure glaucoma. Pain specifically provoked or worsened by eye movement, with decreased color vision and an RAPD, indicates optic neuritis. Severe pain in a contact lens wearer with a corneal infiltrate points to microbial keratitis. When no anterior segment or IOP cause is found, posterior scleritis or orbital inflammatory disease should be considered and may require B-scan ultrasonography or orbital imaging to confirm.',
