    mostLikelyDiagnoses:
      'Bilateral, symmetric disc edema with headache, transient visual obscurations, and/or pulsatile tinnitus in a young, often overweight patient points to idiopathic intracranial hypertension, though any bilateral papilledema requires neuroimaging first to exclude a mass lesion, hydrocephalus, or venous sinus thrombosis before that diagnosis can be made. Bilateral disc edema with markedly elevated blood pressure points to hypertensive/malignant hypertensive optic neuropathy rather than idiopathic intracranial hypertension. Unilateral disc edema with pain on eye movement, decreased color vision, and a relative afferent pupillary defect in a younger patient points to optic neuritis. Unilateral pale, swollen disc with an altitudinal field defect in an older patient with vascular risk factors points to NAION, while the same picture with systemic symptoms of giant cell arteritis (jaw claudication, scalp tenderness, malaise) must be treated as arteritic AION until laboratory testing and biopsy say otherwise, given the risk to the fellow eye. A crowded, anomalous disc with an irregular, lumpy elevation but no true edema, normal vessels, and no associated symptoms, sometimes with visible calcified deposits on B-scan, points to optic disc drusen (pseudopapilledema) rather than true disc swelling.',
