      'Any newly discovered, unexplained disc edema — particularly if bilateral — should be treated as urgent and worked up promptly, since true papilledema from raised intracranial pressure can reflect a life-threatening intracranial mass, hydrocephalus, or venous sinus thrombosis. Bilateral papilledema with any acute neurologic red flag (altered mental status, focal deficit, new seizure, thunderclap headache) is a same-day emergency requiring immediate emergency department referral and emergent neuroimaging. Bilateral papilledema without acute neurologic red flags still requires prompt (same-week, ideally within days) neuroimaging followed by lumbar puncture with opening pressure measurement once a mass lesion has been excluded; confirmed idiopathic intracranial hypertension is managed with weight loss counseling, acetazolamide or other carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, and surgical intervention (optic nerve sheath fenestration, CSF shunting, or venous sinus stenting) reserved for progressive visual loss despite medical therapy. Suspected malignant hypertension requires urgent, controlled blood-pressure reduction in coordination with internal medicine/emergency care. Unilateral disc edema is worked up and managed according to the specific cause identified — optic neuritis, NAION, or arteritic AION — following the same-day emergency pathways described for those conditions, since a swollen disc from any of these causes can herald irreversible vision loss without prompt treatment.',
