    mostLikelyDiagnoses:
      "By far the most common cause of a failed vision screening is uncorrected refractive error — myopia, hyperopia, or astigmatism — confirmed when acuity normalizes with cycloplegic refraction and correction. Reduced acuity in one eye that does not fully improve with correction, often with a history of strabismus, significant anisometropia, or early visual deprivation, points to amblyopia, the leading cause of preventable childhood vision loss and time-sensitive to treat during the critical period of visual development. An eye that turns in, particularly with hyperopia and improvement with glasses, points to accommodative esotropia; a constant, non-accommodative inward turn presenting in infancy points to infantile esotropia; an eye that drifts outward, especially when tired or with distance fixation, points to intermittent exotropia. A white pupillary reflex with or without strabismus, in an otherwise well child, must be treated as retinoblastoma until an experienced examiner excludes it, since early diagnosis is sight- and life-saving. A cloudy lens with absent red reflex present from early infancy points to congenital cataract, and an enlarged, hazy cornea with tearing and photophobia in an infant points to congenital glaucoma — both are visually devastating if not treated within the critical window for visual development.",
