    managementConsiderations:
      "A failed screening or parental concern warrants a full, prompt comprehensive pediatric eye examination with cycloplegic refraction, since amblyopia has a critical, age-limited treatment window and outcomes worsen the longer detection and treatment are delayed — this is not something to defer to a routine, months-away appointment. Uncorrected refractive error is treated with spectacle correction, with re-evaluation of acuity and alignment once glasses have been worn consistently. Amblyopia is treated with correction of any refractive error first, followed by occlusion (patching) or pharmacologic penalization (atropine) of the stronger eye, with treatment intensity and duration guided by the child's age and amblyopia severity. Strabismus is followed for its effect on binocularity and cosmesis, with surgical alignment considered when indicated. Any leukocoria, an asymmetric or absent red reflex, or other findings suspicious for retinoblastoma require same-day emergency referral to pediatric ophthalmology/ocular oncology, since delay affects both vision and survival. Visually significant congenital cataract requires urgent surgical referral, ideally within the first several weeks of life for dense unilateral cases, to minimize irreversible deprivation amblyopia. Suspected congenital glaucoma requires urgent pediatric ophthalmology referral for examination under anesthesia and likely surgical intervention.",
